a peek behind the curtain on Game of  Thrones will reveal a number of things  that may surprise even the show's most  die-hard fans from the special effects  in play to the true life relationships  of the cast and crew these are some of  the most intriguing set images from the  show's production while it should come  as no surprise that the real-life  relationships between professional  actors rarely mirror the characters they  portray it can still throw you for a  loop to discover bitter on-screen rivals  are in fact super great buds such was  the case with Pedro Pascal who played  the fiery doomed Oberyn Martell and Hoth  or us bjornsen who played his sworn  enemy Sir Gregor Clegane they're  shocking duel in season 4 remains one of  the most memorable moments of the series  that's why this picture of the two  actors soaking up raised together seems  so odd  but while the difference between their  on-screen and off-screen relationship  may inspire a double-take or two that  contrast and their real-life physiques  is exactly how they were depicted on the  show with bjornsen towering over his  svelte pal  anyone who has ever spent time on a set  knows the important roles that stand in  sand stunt doubles play these  individuals provide coverage for the  actors between and sometimes even during  filming actress Emilia Clarke stand-in  and stunt double Rosie Mack has received  a good share of media attention over the  course of the show's run thanks in part  to the backstage photos she often posts  on her social media accounts and you've  got to admit all these stand-ins look so  similar to the show's famous leads that  it's actually kind of difficult to tell  them apart at first glance as important  as the actors are and bringing the world  of Game of Thrones to life so too are  the crew members naturally a story this  epic and scope and length requires  hundreds of professionals working  tirelessly at their craft among the most  important of these crew members is  Deborah Riley who serves as the series  lead production designer one of the  reasons the show is caught on with so  many people is because it feels so real  of course it feels surreal because much  of it is many of the grand locales are  shot on location rather than on a stage  or studio backlot  but this illusion is further cemented by  the intricate but subtle set decoration  and design which captures the viewers  attention without being so dazzling as  to distract from the drama of the story  or the performances of the actors you  understand that  you understand the colors you understand  the palette you understand so many  things about the culture that you're  trying to recreate of course not every  locale that appears on Game of Thrones  is the real deal location filming isn't  always an option so in order to achieve  a believable replication the effects  crew will alternate between real and  digital locations this type of visual  trickery is nothing new in and of itself  and Hollywood movies have long used  painted backdrops as stand-ins for  practical scenery but this seamless  blending of outdoor filming with mobile  green screens is just one of the modern  eras most impressive achievements in the  field of CGI given how unforgettable  many of these large-scale scenes have  proven it's fair to say that the show  special effects team has perfected this  kind of digital enhancement sadly not  everything on Game of Thrones can be  done with practical effects in fact one  of the questions fans of the books asked  when the series was first announced was  just how such an epic story could be  realistically adapted to the screen this  was a particular concern since  television wasn't exactly known for  grand spectacle at the time and foremost  among their concerns was the show's  ability to bring Daenerys Targaryen  dragons to life  don't eat the help luckily it turned out  the showrunners knew exactly what they  were doing as a result they've given us  arguably the definitive screen dragons  of the modern era  but for these fantastical creatures  practical effects just weren't practical  and yes this might all look a bit silly  but it really goes to show just what CGI  can really do it's always pretty  remarkable to see the show's actors on  location battling against the elements  such as the case with this image posted  to Instagram by Carice van Houten who  plays the sinister witch Melisandre on  the show she's utterly unflappable she  can stoic ly whether her surroundings no  matter how harsh the conditions thanks  to the power she's achieved through her  practice of the Dark Arts in real life  however then how it is just as  susceptible to the cold is the rest of  us mere mortals it just goes to show  being a successful actor isn't always as  glamorous as it's made out to be one  thing that should come as no surprise to  fans of the show by now is the close  bond shared between various cast members  considering how many characters have  been killed off during the story so far  you've got to imagine this cast member  have probably gotten used to saying  goodbye to one another which is why it's  cool to see they still hang out with  veterans from earlier seasons such as  Jason Momoa who played the late great  Khal Drogo  according to Momoa Instagram account he  met up with his Game of Thrones buds in  Belfast during filming for the final  season of course these pictures sparked  plenty of speculation that the once and  future Aquaman would be returning to the  series in some capacity during its final  season whether that turns out to be the  case or not it's still always nice to  see old friends keeping in touch check  out one of our newest videos right here  plus even more looper videos about Game  of Thrones are coming soon subscribe to  our YouTube channel and hit the belt so  you don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

